{"id":1,"explanation":"The nonce check in verify prevents a token from being reused. Removing it would allow replay attacks. #123 is an issue reference.","unsure":""}
{"id":2,"explanation":"Call ensure_key_salt before constructing KeyManager. If salt setup fails, abort startup rather than initializing the manager.","unsure":""}
{"id":3,"explanation":"Before allocating buf, topN must be no greater than maxSimpleTopN so the resulting slice stays at or below 1 MiB.","unsure":""}
{"id":4,"explanation":"If meta.abi_version is absent, abi must remain None; treating it as the current ABI would be unsafe. #1784 is an issue reference.","unsure":""}
{"id":5,"explanation":"TLP reads /etc/tlp.conf after files matching /etc/tlp.d/*.conf, so settings there override values written by this drop-in at /etc/tlp.d/90-power.conf. Both paths are configuration file locations.","unsure":""}
{"id":6,"explanation":"These TLP settings require version 1.10 or later. _AC, _BAT and _SAV name profile-specific setting suffixes. Leave the _AC setting unset so GNOME’s Performance toggle continues to work; this line sets only the battery setting.","unsure":""}
{"id":7,"explanation":"When JSON parsing throws JsonException, return null from this catch block. The caller is responsible for logging the warning.","unsure":""}
{"id":8,"explanation":"When an AllowedTools set is in use, remove --dangerously-skip-permissions from args because that flag bypasses the --allowedTools restriction. AllowedTools names the tool allowlist; both dashed terms are command-line flags.","unsure":""}
{"id":9,"explanation":"For the dead-code verdict used by thunk_is_dead, consult the verdict table associated with is_definitely_dead_code in rust/xray-core/src/compiler/graph_preamble.rs. The path identifies a source file and the text after the colon identifies a symbol.","unsure":""}
{"id":10,"explanation":"Replace the fixed repos_skipped value with one derived from the domains-to-repos mapping. #874 is the issue tracking that work.","unsure":""}
{"id":11,"explanation":"The regex_query call uses a linear-time DFA and does not backtrack; preserve that performance property when changing the regex search path. DFA means deterministic finite automaton.","unsure":""}
{"id":12,"explanation":"Keep this token-family table schema in sync with RefreshTokenSqliteBackend._ensure_schema in src/storage/refresh_token_backend.py. The path names the source file and the text after the colon names the method.","unsure":""}
{"id":13,"explanation":"The vendor batch API accepts at most 500 items, so MAX_BATCH must stay within that limit. doc:vendor-api#limits points to the limits section of the vendor API documentation.","unsure":""}
{"id":14,"explanation":"Keep these Status values aligned with STATUS_LABELS in web/src/status.ts. Reordering the iota constants changes the meanings of integer values already stored. The path and symbol identify the corresponding labels; #88 is an issue reference.","unsure":""}
{"id":15,"explanation":"Keep tenant_id first and created_at second in this index. That order matches the orders_by_tenant query, whose name identifies the dependent query.","unsure":""}
{"id":16,"explanation":"TList.Add relies on FCount never exceeding the length of FItems. Grow is the operation that preserves this condition when capacity increases.","unsure":""}
{"id":17,"explanation":"Call _flush before closing the sink: closing it drops _buf, so reversing the calls could lose buffered data. _flush and _buf are members used by this close method.","unsure":""}
{"id":18,"explanation":"Because userPath comes from a request, resolve it under workspaceRoot and reject paths that use '..' to escape that root. This applies to the target path constructed here.","unsure":""}
{"id":19,"explanation":"Drain the ring before freeing buf. Otherwise the interrupt handler could use the freed buffer. ring_drain is the required operation; #2201 is an issue reference.","unsure":""}
{"id":20,"explanation":"Reserve space for n items before inserting them to get one allocation. Repeated push_back growth accounted for 40% of the measured profile. doc:perf-notes#ingest points to the ingest section of the performance notes.","unsure":""}
{"id":21,"explanation":"Use HOLDLOCK on this SQL Server MERGE so concurrent upserts do not race. doc:mssql-merge#concurrency points to the concurrency section of the MERGE documentation.","unsure":""}
{"id":22,"explanation":"The autonomous transaction makes an audit row survive even if the calling transaction rolls back. This explains the PRAGMA immediately below the comment.","unsure":""}
{"id":23,"explanation":"SQLite ALTER TABLE cannot drop constraints for this change, so the migration creates a replacement table. doc:sqlite-alter#otheralter points to the relevant ALTER TABLE documentation section.","unsure":""}
{"id":24,"explanation":"The loop scans every five seconds because NFS mounts do not emit the inotify events this code would need to rely on event notifications.","unsure":""}
{"id":25,"explanation":"Keep the for...of loop for awaited jobs. Putting await inside forEach would let errors escape the intended try/catch. The warning applies to how run(job) is iterated.","unsure":""}
{"id":26,"explanation":"A frame stored as Vec<u8> must have length no greater than MAX_FRAME. The decoder is responsible for rejecting larger frames before this value is used.","unsure":""}
{"id":27,"explanation":"Register drainHook before calling pool.start(). On SIGTERM, that shutdown hook drains the pool.","unsure":""}
{"id":28,"explanation":"Move backoff’s retry policy into configuration. internal/retry/policy.go:Default identifies the file and symbol intended to read that configuration.","unsure":""}
{"id":29,"explanation":"Issue #412 contains the full matrix of transitions among the LeaseState values Active, Released and Stale.","unsure":""}
{"id":30,"explanation":"Pass the token to tool login through standard input, as --token-stdin does here. Never put it in command arguments because ps exposes argument lists to all users. TOKEN is the shell variable holding the credential.","unsure":""}
{"id":31,"explanation":"Create app_reader before granting it SELECT access. Migration 042 assumes that role already exists; app_reader is the database role named in both statements.","unsure":""}
{"id":32,"explanation":"Make on_event idempotent because Stripe may retry the same webhook for three days. doc:stripe#webhook-retries points to the webhook retry documentation.","unsure":""}
{"id":33,"explanation":"Compare message authentication codes with hmac.Equal because it runs in constant time; using == would expose timing information. sig and expected are the values being compared.","unsure":""}
{"id":34,"explanation":"Build CodeGraph once per index. Construction costs O(edges), so doing it for each query would repeat that work.","unsure":""}
{"id":35,"explanation":"Changing the packed layout of TCustomerRec would make existing saved .dat files incompatible. Bump FILE_VERSION if its layout changes; FILE_VERSION is the version marker to update.","unsure":""}
{"id":36,"explanation":"Change MSG_MAX together with MSG_MAX_WIRE in proto/messages.h. The path identifies the header file and MSG_MAX_WIRE is the related symbol.","unsure":""}
{"id":37,"explanation":"This handler relies on authMiddleware having set session.userId to a non-null value before the handler runs.","unsure":""}
{"id":38,"explanation":"Synchronize access to cache because the check followed by insertion must be one atomic operation. ConcurrentHashMap alone would not make that combined sequence atomic.","unsure":""}
{"id":39,"explanation":"The repository alias comes from user input, so validate it against the registry before using it in a path join. validate_alias performs that step; #1996 is an issue reference.","unsure":""}
{"id":40,"explanation":"Payment Amount must be nonnegative. Represent refunds as separate rows whose Kind is 'R', rather than as negative amounts in this insert.","unsure":""}
{"id":41,"explanation":"Set the stop flag before joining the worker thread. Otherwise worker_.join() could block forever waiting for a worker that has not been told to stop.","unsure":""}
{"id":42,"explanation":"The limiter is meant to respect GitHub’s API limit of 5,000 requests per hour per token. doc:github-rest#rate-limits points to the rate-limit documentation; the 720 ms interval corresponds to that hourly rate.","unsure":""}
{"id":43,"explanation":"Stop the cidx service before replacing its binary at /usr/local/bin/cidx. Replacing a running binary could fail with 'Text file busy'. The path is the installation destination.","unsure":""}
{"id":44,"explanation":"Do not share this _lease credential lease across jobs: doing so could expose one user’s credentials to another. #77 is an issue reference.","unsure":""}
{"id":45,"explanation":"Keep batch at no more than 999 entries when passing it to executemany; the comment ties this bound to SQLite’s variable limit.","unsure":"SQLite’s variable limit applies to bound variables in a statement. The snippet does not show sql or the number of variables per batch entry, so the reason that len(batch) specifically must be at most 999 is unclear."}
{"id":46,"explanation":"If cgroup.create(limit) fails, log a warning and continue the job. A bad memory estimate must not cause the job to abort.","unsure":""}
{"id":47,"explanation":"Remove the legacy /v1/jobs route. #530 is the issue tracking that work; /v1 is the legacy route prefix.","unsure":""}
{"id":48,"explanation":"Consult the session-model section of architecture decision record adr-007 for why SessionRegistry keeps sessions per tab. doc:adr-007#session-model is the documentation anchor.","unsure":""}
{"id":49,"explanation":"This partial index covers only leases with released_at IS NULL, the active leases. It made active-lease lookup 30 times faster; preserve that filter for the indexed lookup.","unsure":""}
{"id":50,"explanation":"Keep EVENT_TYPES aligned with EVENT_LABELS in web/src/events.ts and with docs/events.md. The first path and symbol identify the corresponding UI labels; the second path identifies the event documentation.","unsure":""}
